From 751062506220b9cb78a078a700bb52265dd589c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ParkerrDev <114211845+ParkerrDev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 22:35:59 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] xwayland: Honour a rootless scale from xwayland_shell_v1

An X11 screen has a single coordinate space and a single DPI for all its
clients, so in rootless mode Xwayland cannot give X11 clients the scale of
the output they are on. Compositors work around it by laying the X11
screen out in output pixels and scaling X11 surfaces themselves, each with
its own handling of surface sizes, input coordinates and cursors, and
with X11 cursors shown at the wrong size.

xwayland_shell_v1 version 2 adds a scale event: the compositor tells
Xwayland the one scale its X11 clients render at, typically that of its
highest-density output. With it, in rootless mode and with wp_viewporter,
xdg-output and buffer damage available, Xwayland:

 - lays the X11 screen out at the logical geometry of every output times
   the scale, rounding the edges so that adjacent outputs stay adjacent;
 - shows every surface it creates at its buffer size divided by the
   scale, through wp_viewport.set_destination (any window size works, and
   fractional scales too, which wl_surface.set_buffer_scale could not do);
 - maps input coordinates back through each window's viewport scale, as it
   already does for resolution change emulation, whose viewport now
   targets the output's size in the compositor's coordinates;
 - shows X11 cursors at their size divided by the scale, also through a
   viewport, so that an X11 cursor of Xcursor.size 24 times the scale is
   as large as a Wayland one.

To the compositor, X11 surfaces become ordinary HiDPI surfaces: pixel for
pixel on outputs of that scale, downscaled on lower ones, never upscaled.
A scale of 1, or a compositor that only offers version 1, leaves
everything as it was. The event is compiled in when the wayland-protocols
in use has it.
